WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.026
A secret matters because of the authority
it unlocks.

2
00:00:04.026 --> 00:00:06.439
A database password permits database
access.

3
00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:09.594
An API key can authorize requests or
spending.

4
00:00:09.594 --> 00:00:11.635
OAuth client secrets authenticate
applications.

5
00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:13.955
Webhook secrets authenticate messages, and
storage

6
00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:15.997
credentials grant access to objects.

7
00:00:15.997 --> 00:00:18.224
Signing keys and other private keys

8
00:00:18.224 --> 00:00:20.636
can prove possession or create signatures.

9
00:00:20.636 --> 00:00:22.956
A certificate usually contains public
information;

10
00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:25.369
its associated private key needs
protection.

11
00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:28.431
These are different mechanisms, but
exposed confidential material

12
00:00:28.431 --> 00:00:31.215
can let someone exercise the application's
capabilities.

13
00:00:31.215 --> 00:00:35.576
An identity names a principal, such as a
user or service.

14
00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:37.804
A credential provides evidence used to

15
00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:40.587
authenticate that principal or authorize a
request.

16
00:00:40.587 --> 00:00:44.392
Many reusable credentials behave like
portable authority: after a password

17
00:00:44.392 --> 00:00:48.197
or bearer token is copied, the receiver
often cannot distinguish

18
00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:52.094
the intended holder from another holder of
the same value.

19
00:00:52.094 --> 00:00:55.156
Additional restrictions can help, but
secrecy alone does

20
00:00:55.156 --> 00:00:57.940
not prove which process sent the request.

21
00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.559
For a TypeScript service, map every place
credentials travel.

22
00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:04.528
Repositories, configuration files, shell
history, deployment scripts,

23
00:01:04.528 --> 00:01:07.034
pipeline variables, logs, crash reports,
support

24
00:01:07.034 --> 00:01:09.911
bundles, and developer chat all create
copies.

25
00:01:09.911 --> 00:01:12.138
Container images and browser bundles can

26
00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.921
distribute those copies much further than
intended.

27
00:01:14.921 --> 00:01:17.891
Deleting a leaked value from the latest
commit

28
00:01:17.891 --> 00:01:21.046
does not invalidate it or erase repository
history.

29
00:01:21.046 --> 00:01:24.572
Revoke or replace the credential and
investigate its use.

30
00:01:24.572 --> 00:01:26.799
The engineering goal is to reduce

31
00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:29.490
credential count, lifetime, privilege, and
distribution.

32
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.088
A secret manager improves storage and
controls

33
00:01:32.088 --> 00:01:34.408
retrieval, yet an application that
retrieves

34
00:01:34.408 --> 00:01:37.192
a reusable password still receives
reusable authority.

35
00:01:37.192 --> 00:01:39.605
Workload identity changes that starting
point.

36
00:01:39.605 --> 00:01:42.574
A trusted runtime establishes which
software is running

37
00:01:42.574 --> 00:01:45.729
and helps it obtain scoped, usually
short-lived credentials,

38
00:01:45.729 --> 00:01:48.884
without a developer installing a permanent
application key.

39
00:01:48.884 --> 00:01:51.854
Prefer proving what the workload is over
distributing

40
00:01:51.854 --> 00:01:55.380
a reusable secret that claims what the
workload is.

